properties.description:
crates/reflow2-core/tests/vocabulary_coverage_reports_what_was_never_used.rs, plus one cross-file tripwire in tests/internal_only_delivery.rs.

THE ORIGINAL FIVE, one decision each: figures always / list only on request; the share computed so the reader is not left dividing; grouped by the schema's own domains, ordered worst-covered first, with ALL eleven reported so a missing domain cannot read as fully used; a domain parkable ONLY by an ACCEPTED ruling, with the remedy stated on the finding; and the barely-started note firing under ten nodes and standing down above.

FIVE MORE, 2026-08-22, for the properties the list now NAMES rather than only counts:
  an unused property is named, node and edge alike, and the naming still rides the WITHHELD list;
  a property is named ONLY on a type that has instances — the vacuous-zero rule applied to the names as strictly as to the figures;
  one instance carrying a property makes it used, without taking the rest of the type down with it;
  a DEFAULTED property can never be named, because the store materialises defaults on write — the honest limit, pinned so nobody upgrades the claim;
  property names come out sorted within their type.

🛑 THAT LAST PROBE WAS WRONG ON ITS FIRST WRITING AND THE SECOND VERSION IS THE POINT. It compared two calls and asserted they matched. The schema is a process-wide OnceLock, so both calls walk the same HashMap with the same seed and agree whether or not anything is sorted — an assertion that could only ever pass. Order has to be checked against something outside the map, so it now asserts ascending property names within each type, which a dropped `names.sort()` kills.

AND THE CROSS-FILE TRIPWIRE INVERTED. `nothing_classified_yields_no_finding_but_the_silence_is_now_reportable` used to assert that `vocabulary_coverage` did NOT name `Artifact.audience`, holding the hole open on the record. It now asserts that it DOES — and separately that the default reply still withholds the list, so 'closed' cannot be quietly rounded up from 'reportable'.

FOUR MUTATIONS RUN AGAINST THE NEW CODE, each killed by exactly its own probe and no other: dropped `names.sort()`; properties named on types with no instances; the property counted but not named (the original hole, restored — killed by the cross-file tripwire); the per-domain figure leaking out of the totals.
